# udev rule of the David server (installed by the package into /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/).
#
# The kernel asks an optical drive every couple of seconds whether the disc has changed ("disk events"). While a CD is being read
# that query makes the drive stop reading and start again, and the rip pumps. The value below is the time between two such queries
# in milliseconds (600000 = 10 minutes); it was measured on the drive of the family server and made the reading even. -1 gives the
# kernel's own default back.
#
# A disc that is put in is then noticed by the kernel only after that time at the latest. The server does not depend on it:
# leave the setting "Detect CD drives with udev" (cd_udev) switched OFF in the CD import tab, then the server looks at the drive
# itself every "cd_poll_seconds". With cd_udev switched on, a new disc is seen late.
#
# To change the value without touching this file, copy it to /etc/udev/rules.d/61-david-cdrom.rules (a file with the same name there
# wins), edit it, and run: udevadm control --reload-rules && udevadm trigger --subsystem-match=block --sysname-match='sr*' --action=change
# To switch it off, make /etc/udev/rules.d/61-david-cdrom.rules a link to /dev/null.
ACTION=="add|change", SUBSYSTEM=="block", KERNEL=="sr[0-9]*", ATTR{events_poll_msecs}="600000"
